/* Lesson quizzes — batch 13 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p35m1":[
 {id:"p35m1q1", q:"A utility does 20,000 meter installs a year; 5% fail, and each failure costs a $200 re-visit. A rule flags 10% of installs and catches 40% of the failures, and every flagged job costs $20 to review. What is the rule's net yearly value?", options:["$80,000","$48,000","$40,000","$160,000"], answer:2, why:"Caught failures: 40% of 1,000 = 400, saving 400 × $200 = $80,000. Reviews: 2,000 flagged jobs × $20 = $40,000, so the net is $40,000. $80,000 ignores the review cost, and $48,000 charges review only to the 1,600 false alarms, but every flagged job is reviewed."},
 {id:"p35m1q2", q:"Your team changes a churn model's output from a 0–1 probability to a 0–100 score. The next morning a billing report and a marketing dashboard show nonsense, and nobody on your team knew either one read the scores. Which hidden technical debt is this?", options:["Undeclared consumers silently reading your predictions","Entanglement, where changing anything changes everything","A correction cascade between stacked models","A feedback loop from predictions into training data"], answer:0, why:"Other systems were reading the scores without your team knowing, so a scale change broke them: undeclared consumers. Entanglement (CACE) is about features interacting inside one model, not about outside systems depending on its output."},
 {id:"p35m1q3", q:"In a dataset where 1% of splices are bad, a new model reports 98.5% accuracy on the test set. What is the right reading of that number?", options:["It is strong, since 98.5% is far above the 50% a coin flip would score","It is fine as long as its 1.5% of errors are spread across both classes","It beats any baseline, because a simple rule cannot catch bad splices","It trails the 99% of always guessing 'good'; use precision and recall"], answer:3, why:"Predicting 'always good' scores 99% while catching zero bad splices, so 98.5% is below a do-nothing baseline. On imbalanced data accuracy hides the minority class; compare precision and recall against the baseline. A coin flip is not the relevant baseline."},
 {id:"p35m1q4", q:"A team trains in a notebook twice a year and emails a model file to the backend team, who deploy it by hand. What does moving to Google's MLOps level 1 add?", options:["A commercial MLOps platform with a monitoring dashboard on top","A training pipeline that reruns automatically on new data, using the same code as experiments","CI/CD that builds, tests and deploys the training pipeline itself, so many models update daily","A bigger GPU so that the twice-yearly manual retraining finishes faster"], answer:1, why:"Level 1 is ML pipeline automation: continuous training on new data with experiment–production symmetry. Building and deploying the pipeline itself through CI/CD is level 2. Buying a tool without the practice is still level 0 with a dashboard."},
 {id:"p35m1q5", q:"A job-recommendation model is retrained every month on click logs, and its suggestions grow narrower each month. Why does adding more of the same logs fail to fix it?", options:["The model is underfitting, and more rows of the same logs will eventually widen what it learns","Click logs carry no labels, so the retraining step has nothing it can learn from","Users can only click what the model showed, so its own predictions shape its training data","The monthly schedule is too slow; retraining daily on the logs would break the narrowing"], answer:2, why:"This is a feedback loop: users can only click what was recommended, so each retrain learns from data the previous model selected. More or fresher logs of the same kind reinforce it; you need exploration or data the model did not filter. Retraining faster only tightens the loop."}
],
"p35m2":[
 {id:"p35m2q1", q:"A colleague checks out the tag v0.3-data. The code is there, but data/ holds only small .dvc files. Which command gives them the exact training table?", options:["git lfs pull, which downloads large files stored in the Git history","dvc add data/, which rebuilds each file from its pointer","dvc repro, which reruns every pipeline stage from scratch","dvc pull, which fetches the cached blobs by hash from the remote"], answer:3, why:"Each .dvc pointer holds the file's MD5 hash and size; dvc pull downloads the matching blob from the configured remote, giving byte-identical data. dvc add works the other way (caches a file and writes a pointer), and Git LFS is not in use in this repo."},
 {id:"p35m2q2", q:"A notebook runs scaler.fit(X) on the full dataset, then train_test_split, then trains a model and reports its test AUC. What is wrong, and what is the fix?", options:["Test-set statistics leaked into training; fit the scaler in a Pipeline on training data only","Nothing is wrong, because scaling is unsupervised and never looks at the labels","The scaler should be fit on the test set after splitting, so that test data is normalized correctly","Scaling should be removed, since tree models and linear models both ignore feature scale"], answer:0, why:"Fitting on all rows puts the test set's mean and variance into the training transform: preprocessing leakage, even without labels. Fit only on training data, ideally with a scikit-learn Pipeline inside cross-validation. 'It never sees labels' is the tempting excuse, but the test distribution still leaks."},
 {id:"p35m2q3", q:"Offline, missing values of avg_loss_30d are filled with the training mean; the production API, written by another team, fills them with 0. Live predictions look nothing like validation. What is this, and what prevents it?", options:["Concept drift; retrain on the newest month of production data","Target leakage; drop avg_loss_30d from both training and serving","Train/serve skew; share one feature definition and replay-test it","Data drift; widen the alert threshold on that one feature"], answer:2, why:"The same feature is computed two ways, so the model sees different inputs than it trained on: train/serve skew. A single definition (for example in a feature store) plus replaying training rows through the live endpoint prevents it. Retraining would not help, because the serving code would still differ."},
 {id:"p35m2q4", q:"Which team most clearly needs a feature store now, rather than a Parquet table with a date column?", options:["Two people scoring one batch churn model nightly from the warehouse","Five models sharing customer features, two needing fresh values at request time","One model retrained each quarter from a frozen snapshot of the data","A research group comparing many algorithms on one fixed public benchmark dataset"], answer:1, why:"A feature store pays off when several models share features and online serving needs fresh values from one definition. A small nightly batch team is the lesson's example of overkill: a point-in-time-correct table with a date column is a fine offline store."},
 {id:"p35m2q5", q:"Two annotators labeling splice photos as good or bad reach a Cohen's kappa of 0.42 on a shared sample. What should you do before labeling the remaining 40,000 photos?", options:["Proceed, because any kappa above zero means better-than-chance agreement","Add a third annotator and take majority votes, which repairs the low agreement","Train anyway and let the model average out the label noise across many images","Rewrite the labeling guidelines and task definition, then re-measure agreement"], answer:3, why:"A kappa near 0.4 says the annotators read the task differently, so the definition is broken; 0.8 or better is the target. Majority voting over an ambiguous task just encodes the ambiguity more confidently, and training on it bakes the noise into the model."}
],
"p35m3":[
 {id:"p35m3q1", q:"You plan a full fine-tune of a 3-billion-parameter model with mixed-precision Adam on A10G GPUs (24 GB each). Roughly how much memory do weights, gradients and optimizer states need, and what is the plan?", options:["About 6 GB, so plain data parallelism on one A10G is enough","About 48 GB; shard states with FSDP or ZeRO across GPUs, or use LoRA","About 48 GB; DDP across four A10Gs splits the load to 12 GB per GPU","About 12 GB, which fits one A10G with room left over for activations"], answer:1, why:"16 bytes per parameter × 3 billion = 48 GB, twice one A10G. FSDP or ZeRO shards parameters, gradients and optimizer states across workers; LoRA shrinks what is trained. DDP does not split memory: every GPU holds a full copy, so each would still need 48 GB."},
 {id:"p35m3q2", q:"A grid covers 6 learning rates × 4 depths × 5 regularization values, at 15 minutes per run on one GPU. How long does it take, and what does the lesson recommend?", options:["About 3.75 hours, which is affordable, so run the grid as designed","About 120 hours; narrow the learning-rate range and rerun the grid","About 30 hours, acceptable because only an exhaustive grid is guaranteed optimal","About 30 hours; use random or Bayesian search with ASHA, 20–50 trials"], answer:3, why:"6 × 4 × 5 = 120 runs × 15 minutes = 1,800 minutes, or 30 hours. Random or Bayesian search with early stopping finds comparable settings in a fraction of the runs, because only a few knobs usually matter. 3.75 hours comes from adding the counts instead of multiplying them."},
 {id:"p35m3q3", q:"A spot instance is reclaimed at step 40,000. After resuming from the checkpoint, the loss briefly spikes before recovering, and the learning rate starts again from its warm-up value. What did the checkpoint leave out?", options:["The optimizer state and the learning-rate scheduler state","The model weights, which had to be rebuilt from initialization","The data version tag, which sets the scheduler's current step","The container image digest, which stores the learning-rate schedule"], answer:0, why:"Weights alone restore the model but not Adam's moment estimates or the scheduler's position, so updates restart cold and warm-up repeats. A full checkpoint also holds RNG states, epoch, step and data-loader position. Missing weights would send the loss back to its untrained level, not cause a brief spike."},
 {id:"p35m3q4", q:"A fine-tune takes 4 hours on one g5.xlarge ($1.006/h on demand, about $0.44/h spot). On a p4d.24xlarge ($21.96/h on demand, about $17/h spot) it would finish in 20 minutes. Which option is cheapest?", options:["p4d on demand at about $7.32, since it runs 12 times faster","p4d spot at about $5.67, because the fastest run costs least","g5 spot at about $1.76, with checkpoints to absorb interruptions","g5 on demand at about $4.02, since spot jobs cannot be resumed"], answer:2, why:"g5 spot: 4 × $0.44 = $1.76; on demand it is $4.02. A third of an hour on p4d costs $21.96 ÷ 3 = $7.32, or about $5.67 on spot. Faster is not cheaper when the hourly rate rises more than the time falls, and checkpointing makes spot safe."},
 {id:"p35m3q5", q:"Two training runs with the same seeds, Git commit, container digest and config give validation AUCs of 0.81 and 0.77 a month apart. The loader reads s3://golden-ml/train/latest.parquet. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The validation set is small, so a 0.04 gap is just ordinary sampling noise","The data was never pinned; 'latest' changed, so log a DVC hash per run","CUDA nondeterminism, which routinely moves AUC by four points between runs","MLflow autologging silently changed the hyperparameters on the second run"], answer:1, why:"Code, environment, config and seeds were pinned; the data was not. A path called 'latest' points at whatever landed most recently, so the two runs trained on different tables. GPU nondeterminism causes tiny differences, not a four-point AUC shift, and autologging records parameters rather than changing them."}
],
"p35m4":[
 {id:"p35m4q1", q:"On a GPU, a model handles a batch of 32 requests in 64 ms, or a single request in 8 ms. What are the maximum throughputs one at a time and with full batches, and what does batching cost?", options:["125/s single vs 500/s batched; a request may wait for the batch to fill","125/s single vs 4,000/s batched, since each request still takes only 8 ms","125/s either way, since a batch takes as long as its requests run in sequence","500/s batched, and per-request latency also drops to 2 ms, so it costs nothing"], answer:0, why:"One at a time: 1 ÷ 0.008 = 125 per second. Batched: 32 ÷ 0.064 = 500 per second. The price is queueing: a request may wait for the batch to form, which dynamic batching bounds to a few milliseconds. 4,000/s wrongly assumes 32 requests finish every 8 ms."},
 {id:"p35m4q2", q:"A field tablet must flag a bad splice photo within 50 ms, often at sites with no cell signal. Which serving pattern fits?", options:["A real-time endpoint behind an autoscaler in the nearest region","A serverless endpoint that scales to zero between inspections","An on-device model in ONNX Runtime or LiteRT on the tablet","Asynchronous inference that writes each result to object storage"], answer:2, why:"With no network and a tight latency budget, the model must run where the photo is taken: edge or on-device serving with a portable format. Every cloud pattern needs a connection the tablet often lacks, and the scale-to-zero serverless option adds cold starts on top."},
 {id:"p35m4q3", q:"A FastAPI /predict handler calls ort.InferenceSession(\"model.onnx\") on every request. A hey load test shows a p99 of 900 ms, although inference itself takes 10 ms. What is the right fix?", options:["Add more uvicorn workers so that the per-request reloads run in parallel","Save the model as a pickle instead, since pickles load faster than ONNX","Report mean latency instead, which better reflects what most users see","Create the session once at startup and reuse it for every request"], answer:3, why:"Loading the model per request adds the full load time to every call; the lesson's service loads once at startup. More workers only multiply the wasted reloads, and a pickle would add a code-execution risk without fixing the design."},
 {id:"p35m4q4", q:"Your end-to-end p99 budget is 150 ms. Network takes 30 ms and the feature lookup's p99 is 25 ms. The model measures 40 ms at p50 and 110 ms at p99. Does it fit?", options:["Yes: its 40 ms p50 fits inside the 95 ms that remains","No: 95 ms remains and its p99 is 110 ms, so it must get faster","Yes: 150 − 30 leaves 120 ms, which is above the 110 ms p99","Yes: averaging its p50 and p99 gives 75 ms, under the 95 ms left"], answer:1, why:"Subtract network and feature time from the budget: 150 − 30 − 25 = 95 ms for the model, and its tail must fit, so a 110 ms p99 fails. Fixes are quantization, faster hardware or a different batch size. Judging by p50 ignores the one-in-a-hundred users the budget protects."},
 {id:"p35m4q5", q:"A 5% canary of v0.4 has guardrails: roll back if p99 exceeds 100 ms or the approval rate moves more than 0.5 points. After two hours: p99 85 ms, errors unchanged, approvals 61.2% versus 60.4% on v0.3. What should happen?", options:["Promotion to 25%, since latency and errors both pass","Promotion, since a higher approval rate means a better model","Automatic rollback, because approvals moved 0.8 points","A switch to shadow mode, which follows any canary alarm"], answer:2, why:"61.2 − 60.4 = 0.8 points, beyond the 0.5-point guardrail, so the canary rolls back even though latency passes: one failed guardrail is enough. A shift in approvals is a business-risk signal, not proof the model improved; that needs labels or an A/B test."}
],
"p35m5":[
 {id:"p35m5q1", q:"A feature's reference distribution over four bins is 25/25/25/25%. This week's traffic is 45/30/15/10%. What is the PSI, and what does it indicate?", options:["0.00, because the gains and losses across the bins cancel out","About 0.32, above 0.25, so it counts as significant drift","About 0.32, but PSI under 0.5 still counts only as moderate drift","About 0.18, in the moderate band, so investigate it later"], answer:1, why:"Terms: 0.20·ln(1.8) = 0.118; 0.05·ln(1.2) = 0.009; −0.10·ln(0.6) = 0.051; −0.15·ln(0.4) = 0.137; total ≈ 0.32. Above 0.25 is significant: act. The differences alone sum to zero, but PSI multiplies each by a log ratio with the same sign, so every term is positive."},
 {id:"p35m5q2", q:"With 2 million rows in each window, a KS test on one feature gives D = 0.006 and p < 0.0001. The feature ranks 40th of 45 in importance. What should you do?", options:["Retrain now, since p below 0.05 proves a meaningful change","Roll back to the previous model until the p-value recovers","Switch to a chi-square test, which copes better with large samples","Log it and move on: D is tiny and the model barely uses it"], answer:3, why:"With millions of rows, trivial differences become 'significant'. D = 0.006 is far below a sensible effect threshold such as 0.1, and drift in a feature the model nearly ignores is noise. Retraining on a p-value alone is the misconception, and chi-square is for categorical features."},
 {id:"p35m5q3", q:"Fraud labels arrive about 60 days after each transaction. A new model went live yesterday. What can warn you of trouble now, and what makes true recall measurable later?", options:["Watch the score distribution and flag rate; log each prediction with an ID and version","Nothing until the labels land; 200 OK health checks and p99 latency cover the gap until then","Compute recall today on the training holdout, which stands in for the live labels","Retrain daily on recent traffic so that the model never has time to go stale"], answer:0, why:"Output proxies (score distribution, share above threshold, business counters) move immediately. A prediction store with IDs, features, timestamps and model version lets you join labels when they arrive and compute recall per version. A healthy endpoint says nothing about correctness, and holdout recall is not live performance."},
 {id:"p35m5q4", q:"Overnight, PSI on cable_length jumps to 0.9 and the model's flag rate falls from 12% to 1%. The source system has started reporting feet instead of meters. What is the right response?", options:["Start the retraining pipeline on the new data so the model learns the feet scale","Raise the PSI alert threshold, since a unit change is not genuine drift","Fall back to the rule baseline, fix the unit in the pipeline, add a contract check","Treat it as concept drift and collect fresh labels before changing anything at all"], answer:2, why:"This is an upstream data failure, not a change in the world. Mitigate with the safe-mode fallback, convert units in the feature pipeline, and add a contract assertion so it cannot recur. Retraining would bake a broken feed into the model and leave mixed units in the history."},
 {id:"p35m5q5", q:"Which of these monitoring signals should page the on-call engineer at 3 a.m. instead of going into the daily report?", options:["A KS p-value under 0.05 on a feature ranked 40th in importance","The feature feed has been stale for 6 hours on a live endpoint","The model is 9 days old against a 14-day retraining target","A PSI of 0.12 on one input feature, inside the moderate band"], answer:1, why:"Page on symptoms that need a human now: a stale feed means live predictions are built on old features. A 9-day-old model is within its 14-day target, PSI 0.12 is investigate-later territory for the daily report, and drift in an unimportant feature is noise that breeds alert fatigue."}
],
"p35m6":[
 {id:"p35m6q1", q:"A hiring screen advances 50% of group A and 42% of group B. True-positive rates are 0.80 for A and 0.66 for B. What does the audit conclude?", options:["Ratio 0.84 fails, because any ratio below 1.0 shows adverse impact","Ratio 0.84 passes, so the model is fair under every fairness metric","Ratio 1.19 passes, because the rule requires a ratio above 0.8","Ratio 0.84 passes four-fifths, but a 14-point TPR gap remains"], answer:3, why:"42 ÷ 50 = 0.84, above 0.8, so there is no adverse impact under the four-fifths rule. Equal opportunity compares true-positive rates, though: 0.80 vs 0.66 means qualified B candidates are found less often. Passing one metric is not passing all; with differing base rates they cannot all hold."},
 {id:"p35m6q2", q:"A team drops age from the model's inputs and declares it unbiased. An audit by age band then finds flag rates of 18% for one band and 11% for the other. What explains this?", options:["Proxy features such as job mix carry age back in; judge fairness on outcomes","The audit is invalid, since age is no longer one of the model's inputs","The gap must be chance, because SHAP would give age zero attribution","Removing age shifted the base rates, so one more retraining will close the gap"], answer:0, why:"Correlated features (job mix, tenure, zip code) let the model reconstruct the protected attribute, so outcomes still differ by group. Fairness is measured on outcomes by group, not by which columns are inputs. SHAP cannot attribute anything to a feature the model never sees, so it proves nothing here."},
 {id:"p35m6q3", q:"You train the same model twice with DP-SGD, once at ε = 1 and once at ε = 8. Which run gives stronger privacy, and at what price?", options:["ε = 8, since a larger budget means more noise is added to the gradients","ε = 1, and it costs no accuracy because the added noise averages out","ε = 1: one record moves outputs by at most a factor of e^1; accuracy drops","Both are equal, because DP-SGD clips each example's gradient the same way"], answer:2, why:"Smaller ε bounds how much any one record can change the output distribution, so ε = 1 is more private; it needs more noise, which lowers accuracy. A larger ε is a looser bound with less noise. Clipping alone gives no guarantee; the noise scale sets ε."},
 {id:"p35m6q4", q:"A public scoring API needs no key and returns raw probability scores. One client sent 2 million systematically varied queries in a week. Which controls address this threat most directly?", options:["Adversarial training on perturbed inputs to harden the decision boundary","Authentication, per-key rate limits and quotas, and labels instead of scores","Differential privacy during training so the model memorizes less about people","Signing the model with Sigstore so that clients can verify its provenance"], answer:1, why:"Systematic high-volume querying is the pattern of model extraction: cloning a model from its answers. Identity, quotas, query auditing and returning less information (labels, not raw scores) raise the cost. Adversarial training defends against evasion and DP against membership inference, not against theft."},
 {id:"p35m6q5", q:"A credit model's average predicted default probability is 0.10. For one applicant the SHAP values are: debt ratio +0.12, late payments +0.06, income −0.03, tenure −0.01. What is this applicant's predicted probability?", options:["0.14","0.28","0.24","0.22"], answer:2, why:"SHAP values are additive: the prediction equals the average prediction plus the sum of all contributions, 0.10 + 0.12 + 0.06 − 0.03 − 0.01 = 0.24. 0.14 forgets the base value, and 0.28 drops the negative contributions."}
],
"p35m7":[
 {id:"p35m7q1", q:"A month's bill: three ml.m5.large real-time instances around the clock at $0.115/h (730 h), 10 hours of ml.g5.xlarge training at $1.41/h, and an idle ml.t3.medium notebook at $0.05/h all month. The endpoint gets a few hundred calls a day. Total, and the biggest saving?", options:["About $302; move the training jobs to managed spot instances","About $51; stop the idle notebook, which is the largest waste","About $302; serve this intermittent traffic from a serverless endpoint","About $302; stop the idle notebook, the single largest cost in the bill"], answer:2, why:"Endpoints: 3 × $0.115 × 730 = $251.85; training $14.10; notebook $36.50; total about $302. The always-on endpoint is 83% of the bill and its traffic is intermittent, so serverless (scale to zero) saves the most. Spot would save at most about $13 on training."},
 {id:"p35m7q2", q:"You are rebuilding the AWS reference platform on Kubernetes with open-source tools. Which mapping of AWS service to open-source equivalent is right?", options:["Feature Store → Feast; Pipelines → Kubeflow; endpoints → KServe","Feature Store → MLflow; Pipelines → Feast; endpoints → Katib","Model Registry → Redis; Model Monitor → lakeFS; Pipelines → KServe","Experiments → Katib; endpoints → Argo CD; Model Monitor → DVC"], answer:0, why:"Feast is the feature store (Redis online, Parquet or a warehouse offline), Kubeflow Pipelines runs the workflow, and KServe serves models on Kubernetes. Katib tunes hyperparameters, MLflow tracks and registers models, Argo CD deploys manifests, and Evidently with Prometheus handles monitoring."},
 {id:"p35m7q3", q:"Which CI check is a directional behavioral test for the QA-failure model?", options:["Changing the technician's name must leave the score unchanged","The frozen test row must score exactly 0.4172 on every build","Validation AUC must match the champion's AUC to four decimals","Raising a job's splice loss must not lower its predicted risk"], answer:3, why:"A directional test asserts the score moves the expected way when an input moves: more splice loss should never mean less risk. The name check is an invariance test, also behavioral but not directional. Exact-score and exact-AUC assertions are brittle and break on harmless retrains."},
 {id:"p35m7q4", q:"In the weekly train.yml run, evaluate.py reports AUC 0.809 against a champion at 0.812, with --min-auc 0.80 and --beat-champion-by 0.005. What happens next?", options:["register.py still runs and registers the run as a challenger for review","evaluate fails, so nothing is registered and the champion keeps serving","The workflow rolls the endpoint back to the version before the champion","A 5% canary deployment starts so the gap can be measured on live traffic"], answer:1, why:"0.809 clears the 0.80 floor but does not beat 0.812 by 0.005, so evaluate fails and the job stops before register.py. No challenger exists, so deployment has nothing new to pick up. Rollback is for a bad deployed version, and canaries belong to the separate deployment workflow."},
 {id:"p35m7q5", q:"The train.yml sketch sets permissions: id-token: write and assumes an IAM role through configure-aws-credentials. Why this design instead of an access key stored as a repository secret?", options:["It lets the workflow commit the trained model file back into the repository","It lets AWS push the evaluation results back into the GitHub pull request","It encrypts the stored access key so that forked repositories cannot read it","Each run gets a short-lived OIDC token that AWS trades for a scoped role session"], answer:3, why:"With OIDC, each run receives a short-lived token that AWS exchanges for temporary credentials on a role that can do only what the pipeline needs, so no long-lived key exists to leak. id-token: write lets the job request that token; it grants no write access to the repository."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p36m1":[
 {id:"p36m1q1", q:"A shop's Postgres serves 3,000 orders per second by primary key. Analysts now run 'revenue by month for ten years' against the same database, and checkout slows whenever they do. What is the right design?", options:["Add an index on order month so the scan becomes cheap enough for the primary","Copy the data by ELT into a columnar warehouse or lakehouse and query it there","Switch the app's database to a columnar engine so both workloads can share it","Schedule the analysts' query for 3 a.m. on the primary, when checkout is quiet"], answer:1, why:"OLTP wants row storage for tiny keyed transactions; OLAP wants column storage that reads only the columns an aggregate needs. Copying the data out to an analytical store is the standard answer. Making the app's database columnar would wreck the per-order reads it exists to serve."},
 {id:"p36m1q2", q:"A table holds 1 billion rows of 25 columns at about 8 bytes each. A query aggregates 3 of the columns. Roughly how many bytes does a column store with 4× compression read, compared with a row store?", options:["About 6 GB versus 200 GB","About 24 GB versus 200 GB","About 50 GB versus 200 GB","About 6 GB versus 50 GB"], answer:0, why:"Row store: 1 billion × 25 × 8 bytes = 200 GB, all of it read. Column store: 3 columns × 8 bytes × 1 billion = 24 GB, compressed 4× to about 6 GB. 24 GB forgets compression, and 50 GB compresses the whole table instead of reading only three columns."},
 {id:"p36m1q3", q:"Two Parquet files hold the same 40 days of events in 40 row groups. In file A the rows are sorted by event_time; in file B they are in random order. A query filters on one day. Why does A read about one row group while B reads all 40?", options:["File B lost its schema when shuffled, so the reader must parse every row","Sorted data compresses worse, so file A's reader can seek to the rows directly","Only file A carries a dt partition, which is what lets a reader skip row groups","A's per-group min/max stats each span one day; every group in B spans all 40"], answer:3, why:"Readers skip a row group when its min/max statistics exclude the filter. Sorted data gives each group a narrow time range, so 39 groups are skipped; random order gives every group the full range, so none can be. Sorting usually improves compression, and partitions are directories, not row groups."},
 {id:"p36m1q4", q:"A team wants to rebuild the nightly crew-footage report as a Kafka and Flink stream to make it 'real time'. Managers read the report once a day, at 7 a.m. What is the best advice?", options:["Stream it, because fresher data is always worth more to the business and its managers","Stream it, because batch jobs cannot be rerun once they have failed","Keep it batch: its value does not decay in minutes, and streams cost more","Use few-second micro-batches, the right compromise for every report"], answer:2, why:"Streaming earns its build, test and rerun cost only when value decays in minutes, as with fraud or alerting. A report read once a day gains nothing from second-level freshness. Batch is the easier of the two to rerun, not the harder."},
 {id:"p36m1q5", q:"Three months after launch you find a bug in the SQL that converts span loss to dB. The stack is ELT, and the raw extracts were loaded untouched. What is the cheapest correct fix?", options:["Fix the SQL and rerun the transformation over the raw data already loaded","Re-extract three months of history from every source system, then reload it","Patch the bad output rows in place with an UPDATE and keep the old SQL","Switch to ETL so that future transformations happen before the data loads"], answer:0, why:"ELT keeps the raw copy inside the platform, so a transformation bug is fixed by correcting the SQL and rerunning it; nothing is re-extracted. Re-extraction is the ETL-era cost ELT avoids, and patching rows by hand leaves the bug in place for tomorrow's load."}
],
"p36m2":[
 {id:"p36m2q1", q:"One task fails with a connection reset while reading from S3. Another fails a contract check because placed_ft is negative. What retry policy fits each?", options:["Retry both up to five times, since retries make a pipeline reliable","Retry the reset with backoff; fail the contract breach at once and alert","Retry neither; every failure should page a human straight away","Retry only the contract breach, since bad upstream data often self-corrects"], answer:1, why:"A network blip is transient, so retry it with backoff. A contract violation is deterministic: the same bad value fails again, so quarantine, fail fast and alert. Blanket retries waste time on deterministic faults, and on a non-idempotent task they can also create duplicates."},
 {id:"p36m2q2", q:"An incremental load pulls rows WHERE updated_at > last_watermark with no lookback, then sets the watermark to the run's start time. Some transactions commit a few minutes late, carrying earlier updated_at values. What happens to them, and what is the fix?", options:["They load twice, so add a unique test on the key and keep the same query","They load normally, since a database always commits rows in timestamp order","They break the DAG's acyclic graph, so split the load into two separate tasks","They are skipped for good; add a lookback window and an idempotent MERGE"], answer:3, why:"A row stamped before the watermark but committed after the run is invisible to the next run's filter, so it is lost. A lookback of a day or two re-reads the recent window, and a MERGE by key makes the overlap harmless. Commit order and timestamp order are not the same thing."},
 {id:"p36m2q3", q:"A Debezium event arrives with op: \"d\", before: {reel_id: \"R-77\", crew_id: 17, ...} and after: null. What should the consumer that applies the stream to a lakehouse table do?", options:["Delete the row whose key is R-77, taking the key from the before image","Ignore the event, since after is null and there is nothing to write","Insert a row of nulls for R-77 to record that the row changed","Re-snapshot the whole table, since deletes break the log's commit order"], answer:0, why:"op d is a delete; the before image carries the key of the removed row, so the consumer deletes R-77 from the target. Ignoring it recreates the blind spot of timestamp polling that log-based CDC exists to close. Deletes arrive in commit order like any other change."},
 {id:"p36m2q4", q:"A producer wants to rename span_loss_db to loss_db in a topic covered by a data contract. What does the lesson say is the right way to ship the change?", options:["As a backward-compatible change, since the column's type does not change","As a forward-compatible change, since old readers ignore unknown fields","As a new contract version, producing both fields through a migration window","Silently, letting the lakehouse's additive schema evolution absorb it"], answer:2, why:"A rename removes a field old readers need and adds one they do not know, so it is breaking under backward, forward and full compatibility. Ship a new version and produce both fields during a migration window. Additive evolution covers new optional columns, not renames."},
 {id:"p36m2q5", q:"Every task in the as-built DAG has been green for two days, yet gold.crew_daily has not changed since Tuesday: someone paused the DAG during maintenance. Which control would have caught this?", options:["An on_failure_callback that posts to Slack when any task fails","A freshness check that alerts if the gold table misses its deadline","More retries with a longer backoff on the S3 sensor task","Setting catchup=True so that the missed intervals run automatically"], answer:1, why:"A paused DAG runs nothing, so nothing fails and no failure callback fires. A freshness check on the output table alerts on the symptom consumers care about, whatever the cause. catchup=True would backfill after unpausing but would not tell anyone the DAG was paused."}
],
"p36m3":[
 {id:"p36m3q1", q:"A 12 GB Parquet dataset is read at 128 MB per partition on a cluster of 4 executors with 6 cores each. How many partitions, and how many waves of tasks, does the read stage take?", options:["96 partitions in 4 waves","96 partitions in 1 wave","200 partitions in 9 waves","24 partitions in 1 wave"], answer:0, why:"12,288 MB ÷ 128 MB = 96 partitions, one task each. 4 × 6 = 24 cores run 24 tasks at a time, so 96 ÷ 24 = 4 waves. 200 is the default shuffle partition count, which applies after a shuffle, not to a file read."},
 {id:"p36m3q2", q:"trips.filter(F.col(\"dt\") >= \"2026-10-01\").withColumn(\"km\", F.col(\"placed_ft\") * 0.0003048).join(F.broadcast(reels), \"reel_id\").groupBy(\"crew_id\").agg(F.sum(\"km\")) — how many shuffles will df.explain() show?", options:["Three: one each for the filter, the join and the groupBy","Two: one for the join and one for the groupBy","One, at the groupBy; the other steps are narrow","None, because Catalyst pushes everything into the reader"], answer:2, why:"filter and withColumn are narrow, and a broadcast join probes a copy of the small table in place without moving the big side. Only groupBy needs all rows of a key in one partition, so a single exchange ends the first stage. Pushdown helps the filter, not the aggregation."},
 {id:"p36m3q3", q:"A stage has 400 tasks: 399 finish in 15 seconds and one runs for 25 minutes. One customer_id owns 35% of the rows. Which change actually shortens the stage?", options:["Raise spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 2,000 so each task gets less data","Salt the hot key or enable AQE skew handling to split its rows","Add executors, since more cores always finish a stage sooner","Cache the DataFrame in memory before the groupBy runs"], answer:1, why:"All rows of one key hash to one partition, so more partitions or more executors still leave 35% of the data in a single task. Salting spreads the hot key over many tasks (aggregating twice), and AQE can split skewed partitions. A stage lasts as long as its slowest task."},
 {id:"p36m3q4", q:"An executor runs with spark.executor.memory=20g, the 300 MB reservation and spark.memory.fraction = 0.6. How large is the unified memory pool, and what uses it?", options:["About 12 GB, set aside for cached data only","About 8 GB, the share kept for user objects","About 20 GB, since the whole heap is the pool","About 11.8 GB, shared by execution and storage"], answer:3, why:"(20,480 − 300) MB × 0.6 ≈ 12,108 MB, about 11.8 GB, shared by execution (shuffles, sorts, joins) and storage (cached data), each able to borrow from the other. The remaining 40%, about 8 GB, holds user objects. Forgetting the reservation gives 12 GB."},
 {id:"p36m3q5", q:"An analyst runs a weekly aggregation over 30 GB of Parquet on a laptop with 32 GB of RAM. Which engine does the lesson recommend?", options:["DuckDB or Polars on the laptop, reading the Parquet directly","A 10-node Spark cluster, since Spark is faster than pandas","Hadoop MapReduce, since the data spans more than one file","Databricks with Photon, since 30 GB already counts as big data"], answer:0, why:"Below a few hundred gigabytes, single-node engines win on speed, cost and simplicity; DuckDB spills to disk past memory and Polars runs lazily on all cores. A cluster pays JVM, scheduler and network costs that a 30 GB job never earns back."}
],
"p36m4":[
 {id:"p36m4q1", q:"A topic receives 2,000 records per second. A consumer group's lag was steady at 1,000 and is now growing by 800 per second. What does that tell you?", options:["Consumption has stopped entirely, so a consumer must have crashed","The producers have doubled their rate while the consumers held steady","The group now consumes about 1,200 per second, short of the inflow","Records are being lost, since growing lag means the log drops data"], answer:2, why:"Lag grows at inflow minus consumption: 2,000 − 800 = 1,200 records per second consumed. Lag growing at the full 2,000 per second would mean consumption had stopped. Lag counts unread records still safely in the log, not lost ones."},
 {id:"p36m4q2", q:"Readings from each of 500 devices must be processed in order. The topic has 12 partitions, and a developer proposes null keys so the load spreads evenly. What should you do?", options:["Key by device_id so each device's records share one partition, in order","Keep null keys, since Kafka orders all of a topic's records by offset","Key by timestamp so records sort by event time across all partitions","Create 500 partitions, since ordering needs one partition per device"], answer:0, why:"Ordering holds only within a partition, and hash(device_id) mod 12 sends every record of a device to the same one. Null keys are spread round-robin in sticky batches, breaking per-device order. Many devices can share a partition; one per device is unnecessary."},
 {id:"p36m4q3", q:"A topic has replication factor 3 and min.insync.replicas = 2, and producers use acks=all. One follower's broker dies. What happens to writes?", options:["Writes stop at once, because acks=all waits for all three replicas","Writes continue, and would still continue with only the leader left","Writes continue, but acknowledged records may be lost if the leader dies","Writes go on with two in sync; one more loss makes them fail, not vanish"], answer:3, why:"acks=all waits for the in-sync replicas, and min.insync.replicas = 2 is still met by the leader plus one follower, so writes proceed and each acknowledged record sits on two brokers. If another replica fails, producers get errors rather than acknowledgments that could be lost."},
 {id:"p36m4q4", q:"A consumer processes each record, inserts it into Postgres, then commits its offset. After a crash some rows appear twice. What makes this pipeline effectively-once?", options:["Commit the offset before processing so a crash never repeats a record","Keep process-then-commit and upsert into the sink by event key","Enable Kafka transactions, which make the Postgres inserts exactly-once","Turn on the idempotent producer, which deduplicates the consumer's writes"], answer:1, why:"Process-then-commit is at-least-once, so a crash replays records; an idempotent sink (MERGE or upsert on a unique event key) makes replays harmless. Committing first is at-most-once and loses records. Transactions and the idempotent producer cover writes inside Kafka, not a Postgres sink."},
 {id:"p36m4q5", q:"One-minute tumbling windows use withWatermark(\"ts\", \"30 seconds\") in Spark. The maximum event time seen so far is 10:05:40 when a record stamped 10:04:50 arrives. What happens to it?", options:["It is dropped: the watermark is 10:05:10, past its window's 10:05:00 end","It is counted, since it is only 50 seconds behind the newest record","It is counted in the 10:05 window, where late records are reassigned","It is dropped, since Spark discards every record that arrives out of order"], answer:0, why:"Watermark = max event time − 30 s = 10:05:10. The record belongs to the 10:04–10:05 window, which ended at 10:05:00, before the watermark, so that window is finalized and the record is discarded. Out-of-order records within 30 s of the maximum are still counted."}
],
"p36m5":[
 {id:"p36m5q1", q:"A query spills on a Medium Snowflake warehouse (4 credits/hour) and takes 20 minutes. On a Large (8 credits/hour) it stops spilling and takes 10 minutes. At $3 per credit, what does each run cost?", options:["Large costs twice as much: $8.00 versus $4.00","Large costs half as much: $2.00 versus $4.00","Medium $1.33 and Large $2.67, as credits double","Both about $4.00; Large just finishes sooner"], answer:3, why:"Medium: 4 credits/h × 1/3 h = 1.33 credits = $4.00. Large: 8 × 1/6 h = 1.33 credits = $4.00. Doubling the size doubles the hourly rate, but halving the time cancels it out. A bigger warehouse costs more per hour, not necessarily more per query."},
 {id:"p36m5q2", q:"A bad write hit a Delta table 10 days ago, and VACUUM runs daily with the default retention. Can you RESTORE the table to the version just before the bad write?", options:["No: VACUUM has deleted files that version needs; rebuild from bronze","Yes: the _delta_log keeps every commit, so any version can be restored","Yes: S3 bucket versioning makes Delta time travel unlimited","No: Delta keeps only the last ten commits, one per checkpoint"], answer:0, why:"The bad write removed files from the table 10 days ago, and VACUUM deletes unreferenced files older than seven days, so the old version's data files are gone even though its log entry remains. The medallion fallback is to rebuild silver from immutable bronze. Bucket versioning tracks objects, not table snapshots."},
 {id:"p36m5q3", q:"A streaming job appends a 2 MB file to a Delta table every 30 seconds. After a month, simple queries on the table take minutes. What is the cause, and the fix?", options:["The log is too long; run VACUUM with zero retention to shorten it","Column statistics go stale; recompute them with ANALYZE TABLE","About 86,000 tiny files; run OPTIMIZE to compact them into large ones","Too few partitions; repartition the table by minute of arrival"], answer:2, why:"Two files a minute for 30 days is about 86,400 files of 2 MB, so listing and opening files dominate every query. OPTIMIZE rewrites them into 128 MB to 1 GB files, optionally Z-ordered. Partitioning by minute makes the small-file problem worse, and zero-retention VACUUM breaks time travel without merging anything."},
 {id:"p36m5q4", q:"dbt build runs a project in which crew_daily is built from fact_asbuilt. A not_null test on fact_asbuilt.reel_id fails. What happens to crew_daily in that run?", options:["It builds anyway, and the failed test is only listed in the run summary","It is skipped, so it keeps its previous contents and bad data stops there","It is dropped from the warehouse until the failed test has been fixed","It is rebuilt straight from bronze, bypassing fact_asbuilt entirely"], answer:1, why:"dbt build runs models and tests in DAG order and skips everything downstream of a failed test, so crew_daily keeps yesterday's data instead of absorbing the bad rows. Plain dbt run would not execute the tests at all, which is why build is the safer command."},
 {id:"p36m5q5", q:"An incremental dbt model selects rows where dt > (select max(dt) from {{ this }}). Tuesday's sheet from one crew lands in bronze on Thursday, after Wednesday has loaded. What happens to those rows?", options:["They merge on (reel_id, dt), because the model sets a unique_key","They fail the unique test, since Tuesday already exists in the table","They are written into Wednesday's partition as the newest data","They are skipped, since Tuesday is not after max(dt); add a lookback"], answer:3, why:"Thursday's run sees max(dt) = Wednesday, so dt > Wednesday excludes Tuesday's rows and they never load. unique_key only governs rows that pass the filter. A lookback such as dt >= max(dt) minus a few days, combined with the merge on (reel_id, dt), catches late uploads safely."}
],
"p36m6":[
 {id:"p36m6q1", q:"Today's gold.crew_daily arrived with 40% of its usual row count, and dashboards showed it at 06:30 because the job writes straight into the consumer table. Which pattern stops this from reaching a dashboard?", options:["Add retries to the load task so it reruns until the count recovers","Run the dbt tests after the dashboards refresh and email the results","Write to staging, audit volume and freshness, then swap it into place","Encrypt the gold table so that only validated data can be read from it"], answer:2, why:"Write-audit-publish writes to a staging table or branch, runs the audits, and only then swaps the result into the consumer-facing name, so a failed volume check never reaches a dashboard. Tests run after publishing only report the damage, and retries cannot conjure missing source data."},
 {id:"p36m6q2", q:"A source sent 12,400 rows for the day. Silver loaded 12,150 of them and the quarantine table holds 180 rows. Does the load reconcile?", options:["Yes: rows in equal rows out plus rows quarantined","No: 70 rows are unaccounted for and were silently lost","Yes: a gap under 1% of the source is normal tolerance","No: none of the 180 rows should have been quarantined"], answer:1, why:"Reconciliation requires rows in = rows out + rows quarantined: 12,150 + 180 = 12,330, which is 70 short of 12,400. Those rows vanished with no reason recorded, the silent loss this test exists to catch. Quarantined rows are accounted for; missing ones are not."},
 {id:"p36m6q3", q:"An analyst's warehouse password is phished, and the account has no MFA. The tables are encrypted at rest with SSE-KMS. What does encryption at rest do against this attacker?", options:["Nothing: a valid login decrypts transparently; MFA and least privilege help","It blocks the attacker, because KMS keys are bound to the analyst's laptop","It forces a fresh KMS sign-in for every query, which stops the stolen login","It hides the rows but still exposes the table names and other metadata"], answer:0, why:"Encryption at rest protects against stolen disks and misconfigured storage; a valid credential is decrypted for transparently. Credential abuse is stopped by SSO with MFA, network restrictions, least privilege and short-lived credentials, the lesson of the 2024 Snowflake customer breaches."},
 {id:"p36m6q4", q:"To honor an erasure request you run DELETE FROM silver.customers WHERE customer_id = 'C-4410' on a Delta table. Is the person's data now physically gone?", options:["Yes: DELETE rewrites the files and purges the old ones immediately","Yes, as soon as OPTIMIZE compacts the table's remaining files","No, and it never can be, since Delta tables are append-only","Not yet: old files keep the bytes until VACUUM; bronze copies too"], answer:3, why:"DELETE commits a new version, but the old Parquet files still hold the bytes for time travel until VACUUM removes them after retention. Copies in bronze and other tables must be handled as well. OPTIMIZE rewrites files but also leaves the old ones in place for time travel."},
 {id:"p36m6q5", q:"The SLO for gold.crew_daily is 'updated by 07:00 on 99% of days'. This year it has missed three times, and today it misses a fourth. What does the lesson's error-budget policy say?", options:["The 99% target allows about 36 misses a year, so nothing changes","The budget allows about three misses; the fourth halts feature work","An SLO miss triggers the contract's financial penalties automatically","Budgets reset every month, so only this month's misses are counted"], answer:1, why:"1% of 365 days is about 3.65, so three misses fit the budget and the fourth exhausts it: feature work stops until reliability is repaired. Penalties belong to an SLA, an SLO written into an agreement with consequences; an internal SLO carries none by itself."}
],
"p36m7":[
 {id:"p36m7q1", q:"A daily Glue job uses 6 DPUs for 30 minutes at $0.44 per DPU-hour; the Flex class costs about $0.29 per DPU-hour. What does it cost per run and per 30-day month, and what would Flex cost per month?", options:["$1.32 a run, about $40 a month; Flex about $26","$2.64 a run, about $79 a month; Flex about $52","$0.22 a run, about $7 a month; Flex about $4","$1.32 a run; Flex is the same price but starts sooner"], answer:0, why:"6 DPUs × 0.5 h = 3 DPU-hours × $0.44 = $1.32 per run, $39.60 over 30 days. Flex: 3 × $0.29 = $0.87 a run, about $26 a month, for jobs that tolerate a delayed start. $2.64 forgets that the job runs for only half an hour."},
 {id:"p36m7q2", q:"On Kubernetes, an executor is set to spark.executor.memory=2g. Memory overhead is 10% with a 384 MB minimum. How much memory must each executor container get?", options:["About 2,253 MB: 2 GB plus 10% overhead","Exactly 2,048 MB, since overhead sits inside the heap","About 2,432 MB: 2 GB plus the 384 MB minimum","About 4,096 MB, since pods need twice the heap"], answer:2, why:"10% of 2,048 MB is about 205 MB, below the 384 MB floor, so the overhead is 384 MB and the container needs 2,048 + 384 = 2,432 MB. Sizing it at 2,253 or 2,048 MB invites Kubernetes to kill the pod for exceeding its memory limit mid-shuffle."},
 {id:"p36m7q3", q:"A two-person data team needs Airflow and Kafka for a modest set of pipelines on AWS. Which approach does the lesson favor?", options:["Strimzi and the Airflow Helm chart on self-run Kubernetes, which is cheaper","Managed MWAA and MSK, since running these systems well is a job in itself","A single VM running cron jobs, since an orchestrator is overkill for two people","Self-managed Kafka with ZooKeeper on EC2 for the most stable possible setup"], answer:1, why:"Operating Kafka and Airflow well is a job in itself, so a small team usually buys the managed service. Kubernetes makes these tools portable and dense, not automatically cheaper, and Kafka 4.0 removed ZooKeeper entirely. Cron lacks dependencies, retries, run history and alerting."},
 {id:"p36m7q4", q:"Last quarter the platform cost $3,000 a month to process 10 TB. This quarter it costs $4,500 a month to process 30 TB. What do the unit economics say?", options:["Efficiency fell, since the bill rose by 50% in one quarter","Efficiency is unchanged, since spending rose in line with the data","Efficiency fell, since each terabyte now needs more compute time","Efficiency improved: cost per TB fell from $300 to $150"], answer:3, why:"Cost per terabyte processed went from $3,000 ÷ 10 = $300 to $4,500 ÷ 30 = $150, so each unit of work costs half as much. A raw bill grows with volume; unit metrics such as cost per TB or per pipeline run show efficiency, which is why the lesson tracks them."},
 {id:"p36m7q5", q:"During an incident, an engineer turns off versioning on the bronze bucket in the AWS console. The bucket is declared in Terraform. What does infrastructure as code give you here?", options:["Nothing, because console changes overwrite the Terraform state","An automatic block on the console change, enforced by Terraform","terraform plan shows the drift; a reviewed apply restores the setting","A rollback of the bucket's objects to their state before the change"], answer:2, why:"The declared configuration is the source of truth, so the next plan reports the difference and a reviewed apply puts versioning back. Terraform neither locks the console nor restores object contents; it reconciles the resource's settings to the code, and changes made outside code are exactly what it detects."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p37m1":[
 {id:"p37m1q1", q:"A page handler makes 40 sequential RPCs to services in the same datacenter. Each costs a 500 µs round trip plus 200 µs of work. What is the floor on the handler's latency, and the usual fix?", options:["About 28 ms; batch the calls or issue them in parallel","About 0.7 ms, since datacenter round trips are effectively free","About 8 ms; only the work counts once connections are pooled","About 28 ms; add bandwidth so each round trip completes sooner"], answer:0, why:"40 × (0.5 + 0.2) ms = 28 ms before anything else happens: 'latency is zero' is a fallacy even inside one building. Fewer round trips (batching, parallel calls, caching) is the cure. More bandwidth does not shorten a round trip, which is bound by latency, not throughput."},
 {id:"p37m1q2", q:"A new data center is 1,800 km of fiber away. Light in glass travels at about 200,000 km/s. What is the physical floor on a round trip, before routing detours and queueing?", options:["About 9 ms","About 12 ms","About 18 ms","About 1.8 ms"], answer:2, why:"1,800 km × 5 µs/km = 9 ms one way, so 18 ms for the round trip. 9 ms is only one direction, and 12 ms uses the speed of light in vacuum (300,000 km/s) instead of in glass. Real paths with detours and queueing land higher still."},
 {id:"p37m1q3", q:"A checkout service calls the payment service, which does not reply within its 2-second deadline. What does the caller actually know, and what should it do?", options:["The payment service is down, so mark the order failed and retry with a new key","The request never arrived, since a processed request always sends a reply","The reply was lost, so the charge succeeded and a confirmation can be shown","Lost request, crash or lost reply look identical; retry with the same key"], answer:3, why:"A missing reply has three indistinguishable causes: the request was lost, the server crashed or is still working, or the reply was lost. The caller cannot know whether the charge happened, so it retries with the same idempotency key and the server deduplicates. A new key on retry is how double charges happen."},
 {id:"p37m1q4", q:"A cluster uses a 100 ms heartbeat and a 1,000 ms election timeout. To fail over faster, a team cuts the election timeout to 150 ms. What is the main risk?", options:["Crashed leaders will now take longer to be detected than they did before","GC pauses or brief congestion will trigger false suspicions and needless elections","None: a shorter timeout makes the detector both complete and accurate","Heartbeats will stop being sent, since they must be under a tenth of the timeout"], answer:1, why:"A timeout is a decision under uncertainty: at 150 ms, a GC pause or a congested link that delays a heartbeat or two makes a live leader look dead, causing churn and split-brain risk. No detector can be both complete and accurate in an asynchronous network; shorter timeouts trade accuracy for speed."},
 {id:"p37m1q5", q:"A backup tool copies files over TCP, which retransmits lost segments and checks a checksum on every segment. Why should the tool still compare a SHA-256 of each file at both ends?", options:["Corruption can occur in RAM, drivers or disk, beyond what TCP checks","TCP's checksum covers only the headers, never the data in a segment","Without a hash, TCP may hand the segments to the app out of order","TCP retransmits only on timeouts, so lost segments are never resent"], answer:0, why:"The end-to-end argument: only the endpoints that understand 'file intact' can guarantee it. TCP's 16-bit checksum protects segments in transit, but corruption in memory, a buggy NIC driver or on disk happens outside it. TCP does deliver bytes in order and does retransmit; that is not the gap."}
],
"p37m2":[
 {id:"p37m2q1", q:"A server's clock drifts at 100 ppm, and its NTP source fails for 6 hours. Roughly how far off can its clock be by the end, and what does that do to last-write-wins by timestamp?", options:["About 22 ms; too small to reorder writes between servers","About 2.2 s; last-write-wins can silently drop newer writes","About 8.6 s; NTP will step it back with no effect on writes","About 0.36 s; only durations timed on this server are affected"], answer:1, why:"100 ppm is 100 µs per second; 6 hours is 21,600 s, so up to 2.16 s of error. Comparing this server's timestamps with another's can then rank a newer write as older and discard it. 8.6 s is the drift over a full day, and a backward step makes ordering worse, not better."},
 {id:"p37m2q2", q:"Process P's Lamport clock reads 4. It receives a message stamped 9, then performs one local event. What values does P assign to the receipt and to the local event?", options:["9 for the receipt, then 10","5 for the receipt, then 6","10 for the receipt, then 10","10 for the receipt, then 11"], answer:3, why:"On receipt the clock becomes max(own, message) + 1 = max(4, 9) + 1 = 10, and the local event increments it to 11. Taking the message's value without adding one would give the receipt the same timestamp as the send, breaking 'if a → b then C(a) < C(b)'."},
 {id:"p37m2q3", q:"Process P2 (the second slot) holds the vector [1,3,0] and receives a message carrying [2,1,4]. What is P2's vector after the receipt?", options:["[2,4,4]","[2,3,4]","[3,4,4]","[3,5,4]"], answer:0, why:"Take the element-wise maximum, [max(1,2), max(3,1), max(0,4)] = [2,3,4], then increment P2's own slot: [2,4,4]. [2,3,4] forgets the increment, and adding the vectors instead of taking the maximum gives [3,4,4]."},
 {id:"p37m2q4", q:"A user's comment is written to the leader, and the page reload is served by a follower 300 ms behind, so the comment seems to vanish. Which fix gives this user read-your-writes without sending every read to the leader?", options:["Have followers resolve conflicts by last-write-wins on wall-clock time","Read from two followers and keep whichever value has the newer timestamp","Return the write's log position; a follower answers only once it replays it","Make every read a quorum read across all replicas, so all of them agree"], answer:2, why:"The token approach hands the client the leader's log position (a PostgreSQL LSN) and lets a follower answer only after replaying past it, waiting or redirecting otherwise. Two lagging followers can both miss the write, and making every read quorum-based buys far more consistency than one user's session needs."},
 {id:"p37m2q5", q:"On a healthy network an app wants the lowest read latency and tolerates stale reads, except for the balance check before a withdrawal, which needs the latest value. What does PACELC thinking advise?", options:["Choose a CP database for everything, since any stale read violates CAP","Read eventually consistent by default; buy strong reads for the balance check","Use eventual reads everywhere, since a balance check rarely hits a stale replica","Nothing special: with no partition, CAP makes every read linearizable"], answer:1, why:"PACELC's 'else' branch is the everyday latency-versus-consistency trade, and many stores let you pick it per request, so buy strong reads only where the operation needs them. CAP says nothing about healthy-network behavior, so it does not make reads linearizable when no partition exists."}
],
"p37m3":[
 {id:"p37m3q1", q:"With asynchronous replication, a leader acknowledged 1.5 seconds of writes that no follower had received, then lost power. A follower is promoted. What happens to those writes, and what would have prevented the loss?", options:["The new leader copies them from the old leader's log as it promotes","They are replayed automatically from the clients' TCP send buffers","They are lost; one synchronous follower would have kept a second copy","They are kept as siblings for the application to merge on a later read"], answer:2, why:"Acknowledged writes that existed only on the dead leader are gone. Semi-synchronous replication, with one follower that must acknowledge before the client is answered, ensures every committed write exists on two machines. If the old leader returns believing it still leads, fencing is needed to prevent a split brain."},
 {id:"p37m3q2", q:"A leaderless store uses N = 3, W = 2, R = 2. What happens to quorum writes and reads when one replica is down, and when two are down?", options:["One down: both succeed. Two down: both fail","One down: writes fail, since W needs every replica","Two down: both succeed, thanks to read repair","Two down: reads succeed, while writes fail"], answer:0, why:"With one replica down, two remain, enough for W = 2 and R = 2. With two down only one answers, so neither quorum is met. Read repair fixes stale replicas after the fact; it cannot supply a missing acknowledgment. A sloppy quorum could keep accepting writes, but it loses the overlap guarantee."},
 {id:"p37m3q3", q:"Two regions both accept writes. Which conflict resolution fits (a) a user's display name and (b) a shopping cart in which items added concurrently must both survive?", options:["Last-write-wins for both, since global tables use it without losing data","A grow-only counter for the cart, since a cart can only ever get bigger","A two-phase set for the cart, so removed items can be added back later","Last-write-wins for the name; an observed-remove set CRDT for the cart"], answer:3, why:"Losing one of two concurrent name edits is acceptable, so last-write-wins is fine there. A cart must keep concurrent adds and still allow removes and re-adds, which the observed-remove set gives with add-wins semantics. A two-phase set forbids re-adding anything removed, and LWW drops one of two concurrent adds."},
 {id:"p37m3q4", q:"A G-Counter has three replica slots. Replica A holds [5,2,0] and replica B holds [3,4,1]. After they merge, what is the state, and what is the counter's value?", options:["[8,6,1], value 15","[5,4,1], value 10","[3,2,0], value 5","[5,4,1], value 15"], answer:1, why:"Merge is the element-wise maximum, [5,4,1], and the value is the sum of the slots: 10. Adding the vectors double-counts increments both replicas had already seen, which is why the merge must be idempotent. Taking the minimum would discard increments."},
 {id:"p37m3q5", q:"One DynamoDB counter item receives 6,000 writes per second at 1 WCU each, and a single partition caps at 1,000 WCU per second. You salt the key with a random suffix. What is the minimum number of suffixes, and what does it cost?", options:["At least 6, and reads can still fetch just the single original item as before","3, since each partition is already replicated three times","At least 6 (more for headroom); reads must fetch and sum every suffix","At least 60, one suffix for every hundred writes per second"], answer:2, why:"6,000 ÷ 1,000 = 6 partitions' worth of writes, so at least six suffixes, in practice ten for headroom. The price is paid on reads: the total is spread across all the suffixed items, so a read fetches each one and sums them. Replicas copy the same writes; they add no write capacity."}
],
"p37m4":[
 {id:"p37m4q1", q:"A coordination cluster must keep working through any two simultaneous crash failures. How many members does it need, and how many if nodes might lie (Byzantine faults)?", options:["4 members for crashes, since two can fail and two still remain","5 for crashes; 7 if nodes may lie","3 members, since a majority of 2 can tolerate two failures","5 for crashes, and 5 also covers lying nodes"], answer:1, why:"Crash tolerance needs 2f + 1: with f = 2 that is 5, and a majority of 3 survives two failures. Byzantine tolerance needs 3f + 1 = 7. Four members have a majority of 3, so they still tolerate only one failure; an even member count adds cost without adding tolerance."},
 {id:"p37m4q2", q:"A Paxos proposer with number 12 receives promises from a majority. Two of the acceptors report earlier accepted values: (n = 7, 'A') and (n = 9, 'B'). What must it send in Domain 2?", options:["accept(12, 'B'), the highest-numbered value reported","accept(12, its own value), since 12 outranks 7 and 9","accept(12, 'A'), the first value any acceptor accepted","Nothing yet; it must rerun Domain 1 with number 13 first"], answer:0, why:"When promising acceptors report accepted values, the proposer must re-propose the one with the highest proposal number, here 'B'. That is how the majority-intersection invariant preserves a value that may already be chosen. Proposing its own value is allowed only when no acceptor reports one."},
 {id:"p37m4q3", q:"A Raft leader sends AppendEntries with prevLogIndex 12 and prevLogTerm 5. The follower's entry at index 12 has term 4. What happens next?", options:["The follower accepts and keeps both entries at index 12","The follower starts an election, since a term mismatch at index 12 proves the leader is stale","The leader steps down, because a follower holds a conflicting entry","The follower rejects; the leader backs up nextIndex until they match, then overwrites"], answer:3, why:"The consistency check accepts only if the follower has an entry with exactly that index and term. On rejection the leader decrements nextIndex and retries until the logs agree, then overwrites the follower's divergent tail, which yields the Log Matching Property. An old mismatched entry says nothing about the leader being stale."},
 {id:"p37m4q4", q:"In a 5-node Raft cluster, the leader answers reads from its in-memory state. A partition leaves it with one follower while the other three elect a new leader and accept writes. What goes wrong, and what fixes it?", options:["Lost writes: the old leader keeps committing new writes with its one remaining follower","Nothing: a leader cut off on the minority side shuts itself down at once","Stale reads: it must confirm leadership (ReadIndex or a heartbeat round) first","Split votes: both sides elect leaders in the same term and must merge logs"], answer:2, why:"The old leader cannot commit writes without a majority, but it can still serve old data as if it were current. Confirming leadership with a majority before reading (ReadIndex, a heartbeat round or a lease) restores linearizable reads. Election Safety keeps the two sides from having leaders in the same term."},
 {id:"p37m4q5", q:"A trip booking reserves a seat through an airline's SaaS API, then charges the card in the payments service, then books the hotel. The hotel step fails. What should happen?", options:["Compensate in reverse: refund the charge, then release the seat, idempotently","Wrap all three steps in two-phase commit so the hotel failure aborts the others","Retry the hotel step indefinitely, since compensations would break atomicity","Compensate in step order: release the seat first, then refund the card"], answer:0, why:"Across independent services, including a SaaS API that cannot join a commit protocol, a saga runs compensations for completed steps in reverse order, each keyed so retries are safe. 2PC needs every participant to speak it and holds locks across the network. Retrying forever leaves the seat and charge stranded."}
],
"p37m5":[
 {id:"p37m5q1", q:"A gateway gives a request a 1,000 ms deadline. Service A spends 30 ms, calls B (200 ms deadline) and C (120 ms) in parallel, then calls D, keeping a 100 ms reserve for its own database write. What deadline should A pass to D?", options:["550 ms","770 ms","670 ms","870 ms"], answer:2, why:"Parallel calls cost the maximum, not the sum: 1,000 − 30 − 200 − 100 = 670 ms. Subtracting both B and C gives 550 ms, forgetting the reserve gives 770 ms, and ignoring the parallel calls gives 870 ms. Propagating the remaining deadline stops D from starting work nobody will wait for."},
 {id:"p37m5q2", q:"Billing and marketing each keep a 'customer' with different fields and rules. An order and its line items must always change together. How does domain-driven design draw this?", options:["One shared customer table for both teams, so the model stays consistent everywhere","Separate services for orders and for line items, coordinated by a saga","One bounded context, since both teams use the same word 'customer'","Two bounded contexts with their own customer; order plus lines is one aggregate"], answer:3, why:"A bounded context has its own consistent vocabulary, so billing's and marketing's customers are different models, often different services. An aggregate is the transaction boundary: an order and its lines change atomically in one service. Splitting them would turn one commit into a saga, and a shared table is a hidden API."},
 {id:"p37m5q3", q:"Match each interaction to a style: (a) browsers and third-party developers read a public product catalog; (b) internal pricing and inventory services in different languages make fast typed calls, some streaming; (c) 'order placed' must reach email, analytics and fulfillment.", options:["(a) gRPC, (b) REST, (c) messaging","(a) REST, (b) gRPC, (c) messaging","(a) messaging, (b) gRPC, (c) REST","(a) REST, (b) messaging, (c) gRPC"], answer:1, why:"Public, cacheable reads by outside clients suit REST over HTTP/JSON. Polyglot internal calls with typed contracts, deadlines and streaming suit gRPC. A fact that many consumers act on later suits messaging, which decouples them in time. gRPC would need grpc-web or a gateway for the browsers."},
 {id:"p37m5q4", q:"A service shares one pool of 100 outbound connections across all its dependencies. The recommendations service stalls for 30 seconds, and checkout endpoints that never call it start timing out too. Which pattern addresses this directly?", options:["Bulkheads: a separate, bounded pool for each dependency","Retries with full jitter on the recommendation calls","A bigger shared pool of 400 connections for all calls","Distributed tracing across the whole checkout path"], answer:0, why:"Slow recommendation calls held every shared connection, so unrelated calls starved. A bulkhead gives each dependency its own pool, so one stall drains only its own compartment. A bigger pool only delays exhaustion, retries add load to the sick dependency, and tracing shows the problem without containing it."},
 {id:"p37m5q5", q:"In Jaeger, a checkout trace ends at the Orders span, and Payment's spans show up as a separate trace with a different trace id. What is the most likely cause?", options:["Payment exports over OTLP port 4318 instead of port 4317","Orders does not inject the traceparent header into its call to Payment","Head sampling at the collector dropped Payment's spans from the checkout trace","Jaeger can show only one service's spans inside a single trace"], answer:1, why:"A trace crosses process boundaries only through the W3C traceparent header that the client injects and the server extracts. Without it, Payment starts a fresh trace. Sampling would drop spans, not create a new trace id, and the OTLP port affects transport, not parent–child links."}
],
"p37m6":[
 {id:"p37m6q1", q:"You need (a) thumbnail jobs, each handled by any one of 200 workers with per-message retry and delay, and (b) order events read by six teams, with replay for services added later. Which fits each?", options:["A log for both, since a queue cannot feed 200 consumers","A queue for both, since fan-out to six teams is a queue's strength","A log for the thumbnails and a queue for the order events","A queue for the thumbnails and a log for the order events"], answer:3, why:"Task distribution with per-message acknowledgement, retry and delay is what queues do, with any number of competing consumers. Many independent readers, each with its own position and the ability to rewind, is what a log does. A classic log caps parallelism at the partition count and offers no per-message delay."},
 {id:"p37m6q2", q:"A topic must carry 240 MB/s. One partition handles about 40 MB/s on the producer side and 15 MB/s per consumer. How many partitions do you need at minimum, and why add headroom now?", options:["16, plus headroom, because adding partitions later remaps keys","6, since the producer side alone sets the partition count","16 exactly, since partitions can be added later with no side effects","22, the sum of the producer and consumer requirements"], answer:0, why:"Partitions ≥ max(240 ÷ 40, 240 ÷ 15) = max(6, 16) = 16; the slower consumer side decides. Choose about 20–24, because adding partitions later changes hash(key) mod n and breaks per-key order for existing data. Summing the two requirements double-counts."},
 {id:"p37m6q3", q:"A topic has RF = 3 with min.insync.replicas left at its default of 1, and producers use acks=all. Both followers fall out of the ISR, the leader keeps acknowledging writes, and then its disk fails. What happens?", options:["Nothing is lost, because acks=all always waits for all three replicas","Writes acknowledged while the ISR was the leader alone are lost","Producers got NotEnoughReplicas errors, so nothing was ever acknowledged","The followers already hold those writes, since they pull every record"], answer:1, why:"acks=all waits for the current ISR, and with min.insync.replicas = 1 that can be the leader alone, so acks=all quietly became acks=1. NotEnoughReplicas would have stopped writes only with a minimum of 2. Out-of-sync followers are exactly the ones that lack the recent records."},
 {id:"p37m6q4", q:"A read-model projection keeps a count of paid orders in PostgreSQL. After a rebalance it receives events 1,040–1,045 again, which it had already applied. What keeps the count correct?", options:["Enable read_committed so that redelivered events are filtered out","Rely on Kafka's exactly-once transactions, which also cover the PostgreSQL update","Save the last applied position in the view's transaction; skip events at or below it","Commit the consumer offset before applying each event to the view"], answer:2, why:"Projections are consumers and see redeliveries after rebalances, so they must be idempotent: store the last applied position atomically with the view and ignore anything at or below it. read_committed hides aborted records, not redelivered ones, and exactly-once does not extend to a PostgreSQL sink."},
 {id:"p37m6q5", q:"A consumer fetches 500 records per poll, and each takes about 1 second to process. Its group keeps rebalancing and lag climbs; max.poll.interval.ms is at its 5-minute default. What is happening, and what helps?", options:["The brokers are overloaded; add brokers so that fetches return faster","Offsets are committed too often; commit them once an hour instead","There are too few partitions; adding some makes each record faster","Each batch takes ~500 s, past the 300 s limit; lower max.poll.records"], answer:3, why:"500 records × 1 s = 500 s between polls, beyond the 300 s limit, so the consumer is evicted, its partitions rebalance and uncommitted work is redone. Fewer records per poll (or a bounded worker pool with pause and resume) keeps polls frequent. Partitions add parallelism but do not speed up each record."}
],
"p37m7":[
 {id:"p37m7q1", q:"At peak a service needs 120 fully busy cores. The fleet targets 60% utilization and must survive the loss of one of three availability zones. How many cores should you provision?", options:["About 200 cores","About 300 cores","About 180 cores","About 400 cores"], answer:1, why:"120 ÷ 0.6 = 200 cores at the target utilization; surviving a zone loss means the two remaining zones carry everything, so × 1.5 = 300. 200 forgets the zone loss, 180 forgets the utilization headroom, and doubling over-buys for losing one zone of three."},
 {id:"p37m7q2", q:"A service runs on 10 hosts, and each customer is assigned a random pair of them (shuffle sharding). A poison customer takes down its pair. How many distinct pairs exist, and what share of customers lose both of their hosts?", options:["20 pairs; about 1 in 20 customers lose both hosts","10 pairs; every customer sharing either host is down","90 pairs; about 1 in 90 customers lose both hosts","45 pairs; about 1 in 45 customers lose both hosts"], answer:3, why:"10 choose 2 = 10 × 9 ÷ 2 = 45 distinct pairs, so only customers assigned the same pair, about 1 in 45, lose both hosts; others sharing one host keep the other. 90 counts each pair twice by order. That small blast radius is the point of shuffle sharding."},
 {id:"p37m7q3", q:"With cache-aside, reader A misses and reads row v1 from the database. Writer B then updates the row to v2 and deletes the cache key. A now stores v1 in the cache. What is the effect, and what fixes it?", options:["v1 stays until its TTL; a lease issued on the miss and voided by the delete fixes it","Nothing harmful, since B's delete has already removed the key from the cache for good","It heals on B's next read, which overwrites v1 with v2 straight away","Setting the key on write instead of deleting it, which is always race-free"], answer:0, why:"A's set lands after B's delete, so the stale value survives until it expires. Facebook's fix gives the missing reader a lease token that a delete invalidates, so A's late set is refused; commit-log-driven deletes stop forgotten invalidations. Reads hit the cached v1, so nothing heals it early."},
 {id:"p37m7q4", q:"gRPC clients reach a backend fleet through an L4 network load balancer. One backend runs at 90% CPU while the others sit nearly idle, and adding backends does not help. What is the likely cause?", options:["Round robin spreads traffic unevenly when instance sizes differ","The busy backend lacks slow start, so its cold caches never warm","Each client's long-lived HTTP/2 connection is pinned to one backend","Power of two choices always favors the lowest-numbered backend"], answer:2, why:"gRPC multiplexes every call over one long-lived HTTP/2 connection, and an L4 balancer routes connections, not requests, so a heavy client stays on one backend. Use L7 or client-side balancing that spreads individual calls. New backends receive only new connections, which is why adding them did nothing."},
 {id:"p37m7q5", q:"A team wants to test whether checkout survives the loss of an availability zone. Which plan follows the principles of chaos engineering?", options:["Stop one zone across every cell at once, since a partial test cannot show real behavior","Define a steady-state SLI and hypothesis, fail one zone in a 5% cell, stop on SLO breach","Run it in staging only, forever, since production experiments break the SLO by definition","Kill random instances in one cell with no hypothesis, and record whatever changes"], answer:1, why:"The method is steady state, hypothesis, a real-world fault, a minimal blast radius with a stop condition, and automation. Failing every cell at once maximizes damage without adding knowledge, and an experiment without a hypothesis cannot be confirmed or refuted. Production game days are the goal once staging passes."}
]
});
